nodecode-fleet-policy is a rewrite of Julientalbot's Julientalbot/hermes-fleet-policy:

  https://github.com/Julientalbot/hermes-fleet-policy
  commit 7d36874a5a625fc2a0c24ee280e34872e5259800

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Julien Talbot (LICENSE)

Written from scratch in Common Lisp, from the behaviour of the original's
`fleet_policy/watch.py`, `store.py` and `extract.py`: one tool call on one MCP
server over streamable HTTP, the id field searched at any depth of the answer
(embedded JSON documents included), the envelope's failure rule, the baseline
on the first run, the gate line when nothing is new, the bounded set of seen
ids written whole through a temp file and a rename, and a corrupt state that
refuses rather than start over.

Left out, because Nodecode already answers them or cannot use them: the
managed-scope comparison (`status`, the sha256 of a managed config.yaml, the
effective dotted keys) and the observation hooks that record the model,
reasoning effort and served service tier per role, which read Hermes's own
hook and middleware surfaces and its log store; the cron audit of model and
effort pins; and the per-job cost report (`crons --cost`), which reads Hermes's
`state.db` and `executions.db` for runs, empty shares and dollars. The two
bundled skills (administering a managed policy, setting up watches) are folded
into this README and the cell's manual topic instead of being registered as
skills. What remains is the watch, reimplemented as one verb the model calls,
one tool on the wire, and one operator command.
